2. 🤿 Another day, another brutal tech dive
 
 By Matt Phillips 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: FactSet; Chart: Matt Phillips/Axios AI and tech took another thumping yesterday.

 The big picture: Ongoing uncertainty surrounding the impact of Huang's departure on the AI trade seemed to combine with jitters about Anthropic's IPO (due Thursday) to take down tech stocks. 

 By the numbers: Chips were hit hardest, as the SOX semiconductor index tumbled 5.3%. (The SOX was down 34.4% from its 52-week high at the end of yesterday's session. )

 The Nasdaq 100 index of large-cap technology shares shed 2.7%. The S&amp;P 500 lost 1.9%. Zoom in: Losses were particularly deep for stocks linked to the AI build-out.

 Memory device maker Sandisk — a market favorite last year — dove 8.6%. Fiber-optic cable connection maker Lumentum, which also soared last year, plunged 8.5%. AI infrastructure plays GE Vernova — which makes turbines for power plants — and construction equipment maker Caterpillar both dropped nearly 5%. What they're saying: AI bears are currently focused on three themes — the massive pushback against data centers in the midterm elections , declines in AI token pricing and the persistent growth in debt needed to finance the AI build-out — says Jake Manoukian, head of of U.S. Investment Strategy at J.P. Morgan Private Bank. 

 "We're just in a moment where all three of the bear cases are kind of coalescing together," Manoukian told Axios. "But on the other hand, that's leading us to an opportunity, because the valuation premium in the AI theme has evaporated." The bottom line: This week remains a giant test of the AI trade, and the only way to get through it, is to go through it. 


 
 
